`cast expression` needs a target from its immediate concrete receiving
boundary. The target does not choose the source type. See
@./#cast-syntax (Cast syntax) for the boundary rules.

### Builtin targets

The eleven fixed numeric targets are:

- Integers: `I8`, `I16`, `I32`, `I64`, `U8`, `U16`, `U32`, `U64`
- Binary floats: `F16`, `F32`, `F64`

Builtin conversion policies also cover `Byte`, `Dec`, `Dec0`, `Dec1` through
`Dec256`, `Bool`, `String`, `Char` and `Error`. A target name being valid does
not make every source-target pair valid.

### Fixed numeric conversions

Fallibility follows the complete source and target types, not a known value:

- Fixed integer-to-integer conversion preserves the value. It is infallible when the complete source range fits the target and fallible otherwise.
- Fixed integer-to-`F32` or `F64` conversion is infallible and may round. Conversion goes directly to the destination precision.
- Fixed integer-to-`F16` is infallible only when the complete source range fits the finite `F16` range. Otherwise it is fallible.
- Binary-float precision widening is infallible. Narrowing is fallible because a rounded result may become non-finite. Round directly to the destination, nearest with ties to even. Precision loss alone succeeds.
- Binary-float-to-integer conversion is fallible. Truncate towards zero, then check the target range. `-0.5` truncates to zero and can convert to an unsigned target; `-1.0` fails.

For example, `U8 -> U16` is infallible, `U16 -> U8` is fallible and
`F64 -> F16` is a direct narrowing conversion, never an `F64 -> F32 -> F16`
chain. `U64 -> F32` converts directly without an intermediate binary64
rounding step.

For the complete `U64` value `9007199791611905`, direct conversion to `F32`
produces bits `0x5a000001`. An intermediate `F64` conversion would collapse
the value to `0x5a000000`.

Integer-to-`Dec` and scale widening are exact and infallible. Scale narrowing
and `Dec`-to-integer conversions are exact or fail: removed fractional digits
must be zero and the full integer value must fit its target. `Dec` does not
convert directly to or from a binary float.

`Byte` and `U8` convert infallibly in both directions. Other numeric
conversions involving `Byte` compose through `U8`. `Byte` remains distinct
from `U8`; no receiving boundary silently changes one into the other.

`Byte` has no direct conversion to `Bool` or `Char`.

### Text and other builtin conversions

- `String` parses the complete numeric grammar for fixed integers, binary floats, `Byte` and `Dec` targets. These conversions can fail.
- Every numeric type formats to `String` infallibly using its canonical text. `Byte` composes through `U8`.
- `String -> Bool` trims surrounding whitespace and accepts only lowercase `true` or `false`. It can fail.
- `String -> Char` requires exactly one Unicode scalar and can fail.
- `Char -> I32` returns the Unicode scalar code point. `I32 -> Char` can fail if the value is not a Unicode scalar.
- `Bool -> String` and `Char -> String` are infallible.
- `String -> Error` creates an error message with code `0`. `Error -> String` returns only the message. Both are infallible.

Same-type casts are invalid. No cast acts as an identity conversion.

No other source-target pair follows from this list. In particular, `Dec` and
binary floats have no implicit mixing or direct cast pair.

### Exactness and numeric text

A cast does not promise universal exactness. Integer-to-integer conversions
preserve values. Binary-float conversions round to nearest, ties-to-even.
Binary-float-to-integer conversions truncate towards zero before the range check.
Decimal scale narrowing and decimal-to-integer conversions require exactness
and never round.

Numeric text consumes the whole string. Parsing rejects surrounding whitespace,
unary `+`, uppercase exponent markers, malformed separators or exponents,
`NaN` and infinity spellings. Unsigned integer targets also reject a leading
minus, including `-0`. A grammar-valid float spelling still fails if its
rounded result becomes non-finite.

Binary-float formatting normalises negative zero to `0` and uses lowercase
exponents when exponential notation is needed. Decimal formatting trims
fractional trailing zeroes, uses a leading zero before fractions and never
emits exponent notation or negative zero. Unsigned integer formatting uses
base ten and writes zero as `0`.

No modulo, wrapping or saturating conversion exists.

### Optional receiving contexts

A `T?` receiver casts to inner `T`, then applies ordinary optional wrapping.
The recovery handler also produces `T`; it cannot use `then none`. Optional
source values never unwrap automatically.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    parse_optional |text String| -> I32?:
        value I32 = cast text catch:
            return none
        ;
        return value
    ;
]

The Progress matrix owns current compiler and backend support:
@../progress (Progress).
